{"t":"2026-11-22T22:28:27.068Z","i":11,"do":"waitReply","expect":"a long answer streams in; it is readable, not cut off, and the buttons under it never cover the words","timeline":[{"at":50,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":5240,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":false,"streaming":1},{"at":11211,"badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":false,"streaming":0}],"watchShots":[{"at":1625,"mark":1500,"shot":"21-long-wait-1.5s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":3079,"mark":3000,"shot":"22-long-wait-3.0s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":true,"streaming":0},{"at":6091,"mark":6000,"shot":"23-long-wait-6.0s.png","badges":[],"reactions":[],"thinking":false,"streaming":1}],"firstReplyMs":5213,"newBubbles":1,"reactions":[],"ok":true,"duplicates":0,"lastReply":"Sher Khan, a tiger in the Sundarbans, was tired of eating the same plain dinner every night. One evening a warm, spicy smell drifted in from a village wedding across the river, and he followed his nose to a huge pot of biryani. He sat hidden in the reeds until dawn, watching the old cook layer rice and masala, and decided he had to learn.\n\nHis first try was a disaster. He burnt the onions, used a whole fistful of chillies, and the rice turned into sticky paste that made the monkeys laugh from the trees. But the cook's granddaughter, Meera, spotted him sneezing over his pot and, instead of running, showed him the secrets: soak the rice, fry the onions slow and golden, add saffron milk, then seal the lid and wait.\n\nWaiting was the hardest part for a tiger, but Sher Khan sat still for the full dum, tail twitching. When he lifted the lid, the steam smelled like the wedding night. He shared the pot with Meera, the monkeys and even a nervous deer, and from then on the jungle knew him not for his roar but for his Sunday biryani. 🐯\n\n3:58 AM","kit":[],"replyFaults":[],"ms":17263,"shot":"24-long.png"}
